So I got in trouble for Nethack at work.  Not playing it, mind you; I
don't really play it here, and they wouldn't necessarily have a way of
tracking it.  But just now my boss and some guy I've never seen before
(who turns out to be from the computer security division) come into my
office, look at the identification number of the front of my computer,
and the guy says, "Yup, this is the one."

He then proceeds to tell me I've been doing some unauthorized
activities.  I'm thinking ... well crap.  I've made sure never to do
any filesharing and I don't visit any questionable websites (because I
KNOW they're going to be packet-sniffing), so what is this about?  He
tells me, "You've been using IRC, which technically isn't against the
rules, but our records show you've been on the channel #nethack."  Oh
shit, I think.  How am I going to explain this.  I said, "Umm, well
actually it's a game."  He didn't look as if he believed me.  "Well
games are off-limits too," he says.  I say, "Well actually, I don't
play a game on there, it's just a chatroom about a game that I have
friends on and I like to chat with occasionally."

Well anyway, he made me uninstall my IRC client.  I always thought it
funny before when some would-be hacker would enter #nethack looking for
hacking information, but now it's turned around and bitten me on the
ass because my employers think it's an illicit activity too.  And now
my boss is slightly amused, slightly mad at me.  Gahhhrrrr.

Anyone know of a good command-line IRC client?  I guess I'll SSH into
my home computer and run IRC from there.  Because not having the
occasional distraction while attempting to program for eight hours
straight is NOT gonna work.


